      "math_academy_id": "e-3635",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
